{% if use_kb %}Claude's persistent memory for this project lives in
[kb](https://github.com/okolovmark/kb): records, tasks and one record per session, in Neo4j.
The SessionStart hook opens the session and prints the index and the standup. kb is user-level:
one binary in your nix profile, one Neo4j user service and one database for every project on the
machine. One command makes sure of all of it for this checkout:

```bash
nix run .#kb-setup                # install kb when missing, run kb-setup, register this checkout
nix run .#kb-setup -- --upgrade   # the same, taking the latest kb first
```

It installs `github:okolovmark/kb` into your user profile when kb is missing. Then it runs kb's
own `kb-setup`, which starts the `neo4j-kb` user service (about 1.4 GB of RAM) and puts kb's Claude
Code plugin on `CLAUDE_CODE_PLUGIN_DIRS`. Last, it registers this checkout as the kb scope
`{{ project_name }}` with `kb scope add`, so records made here land in it and `.kb/tags.toml` is read.
It is safe to re-run. Until kb is installed and the checkout registered, every session start says so
in one line.

A markdown memory from before kb (`state.md`, `nodes/`, `journal/` in Claude's memory folder for
this project) is imported once with `kb migrate state`, then `nodes`, then `journal`, each with
`--scope {{ project_name }}` and a `--dry-run` first: the
[migration guide](https://github.com/okolovmark/kb/blob/main/docs/migration.md).
{% elif use_claude_code %}Initialize Claude's persistent per-project memory once (index, journal, state,
bootstrap questions — see `.claude/memory-template/README.md`):

```bash
bash .claude/memory-template/scripts/init-memory.sh
