### Entire CLI installation

Sessions here are recorded by [Entire](https://entire.io): checkpoints on `refs/entire/*`,
one session record per repository. The `.claude/` hooks call it on every phase and carry on
silently when it is missing, so nothing breaks without it.

Prefer the release artifact over the vendor's one-line installer, which pipes a script from
the network straight into a shell. The binary is a static Go build, so unpacking is all it
takes:

```bash
gh release download v0.11.5 --repo entireio/cli \
  --pattern 'entire_linux_amd64.tar.gz' --pattern checksums.txt
sha256sum -c --ignore-missing checksums.txt
tar -xzf entire_linux_amd64.tar.gz entire git-remote-entire
install -m 755 entire git-remote-entire ~/.local/bin/
```

The version is not pinned by the flake: it lives on each machine, like Claude Code itself,
and the CLI nudges you when a newer one exists. 0.11.5 is what this project has been
verified against; check yours with `entire --version`, and keep the pair you replace for a
rollback.

### Entire and git auto-gc

Every session pushes a checkpoint, so `refs/entire/*` and the loose-object count grow with use.
Past `gc.auto` (6700 loose objects) any git command fires `git maintenance run --auto`, and on a
tree that size an unbounded `git repack` is heavy enough to take the machine down. Measured on
this project at 1325 `refs/entire/*` and 76,636 loose objects (17 GB of a 19 GB `.git`): 16 GB
RSS and ~600 % CPU, which OOM-killed the editor whose git call had launched it. The repack dies
with the editor, nothing gets packed, and the next editor start repeats it from scratch.

Bound the repack and keep it out of the editor's process tree, in every checkout entire
records{% if custom_repo_name %} — with `src/{{ custom_repo_name }}` nested, that is two{% endif %}:

```bash
git config maintenance.auto false     # no repack inside an editor's own git call
git config pack.threads 2
git config pack.windowMemory 256m
git config pack.deltaCacheSize 128m
```

That leaves nothing packing automatically, so pack deliberately when the loose count climbs into
the tens of thousands (`git count-objects -v`), capped, as a transient unit that outlives the
editor:

```bash
systemd-run --user --unit="git-gc-$(basename "$PWD")" --collect \
  --property=MemoryMax=8G --property=MemorySwapMax=0 \
  --property=CPUQuota=250% --property=Nice=10 \
  --property=IOSchedulingClass=idle \
  --working-directory="$PWD" \
  "$(command -v git)" gc --no-detach
```

Read the run's real memory off `anon` in the unit's `memory.stat`, which stays below 1 GB under
these limits. `MemoryCurrent` is not that figure: it sits near the cap throughout as mmap'd pack
cache the cgroup reclaims, so `memory.events` counts `max` hits at `oom_kill 0`. The measured
tree above took 28 minutes of wall clock and came out at an 11 GB `.git` — 54 loose objects in
2 packs, `git fsck --connectivity-only` clean, every `refs/entire/*` intact.

