## Production Server

### Quick Setup For Production

```bash
cd {{ project_name }}

# Full production setup
nix run .#setup-prod

# Lock python dependencies from src/odoo/requirements.txt
nix run .#bootstrap-deps

# Install to user profile
{% if service_suffix %}mkdir -p ~/.local/state/nix/profiles && {% endif %}nix profile add {{ nix_profile_flag }}.#prod-server

# Enable running when not logged in
sudo loginctl enable-linger $USER

# Odoo service + log rotation
systemctl --user enable odoo{{ service_suffix }}.service odoo{{ service_suffix }}-logrotate.timer
systemctl --user start odoo{{ service_suffix }}.service odoo{{ service_suffix }}-logrotate.timer
systemctl --user status odoo{{ service_suffix }}.service
```

This profile ships neither a reverse proxy nor the Postgres client: put your
own proxy in front of Odoo on `{{ odoo_http_port }}` (and
`{{ odoo_gevent_port }}` for websockets), and note that Odoo's own database
manager (backup, duplicate, restore) shells out to `pg_dump`/`pg_restore`, so
it needs a Postgres client on `PATH` from somewhere else if you use it.

